## Las Meninas: who is the painter painting?
sources:
    - id: prado
      title: Las Meninas, or The Family of Philip IV (P001174)
      authors: Museo del Prado, Madrid
      year: 2024
      finding: Velázquez, 1656, oil on canvas, 318 × 276 cm; the infanta Margarita, her meninas, dwarfs, the king and queen in the mirror
    - id: wiki
      title: Las Meninas
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Figures identified after Palomino (1724); the mirror''s two readings (the royal couple in the viewer''s place, or a reflection of the canvas: Janson, Snyder); the Santiago cross received 1659, added on Philip''s orders; painting seen as a craft; titled Las Meninas only from 1843'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Meninas
    - id: palomino
      title: El Parnaso español pintoresco laureado
      authors: Antonio Palomino
      year: 1724
      finding: 'The earliest detailed description: the sitters'' names; the cross added after Velázquez''s death, ''some say his Majesty himself painted it'''
    - id: stratton
      title: Velázquez's Las Meninas
      authors: Suzanne L. Stratton-Pruitt (ed.)
      year: 2003
      finding: The 1666 inventory places the painting in the king's private summer quarters of the Alcázar

    card title "Las Meninas"

Diego Velázquez painted this enormous canvas, more than three metres tall, at the Spanish court in 1656.
    image art cites ["prado","wiki"] credit "Diego Velázquez, 1656. Museo del Prado, Madrid. Public domain." x 315 y 342 width 550 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Las_Meninas%2C_by_Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez%2C_from_Prado_in_Google_Earth.jpg/1920px-Las_Meninas%2C_by_Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez%2C_from_Prado_in_Google_Earth.jpg look screen regions.canvas [0,0.14,0.175,0.84] regions.chaperone_guard [0.66,0.51,0.2,0.16] regions.cup [0.37,0.71,0.04,0.04] regions.dog [0.6,0.835,0.32,0.14] regions.doorway [0.5,0.52,0.115,0.15] regions.eyes_band [0.17,0.5,0.7,0.21] regions.front [0,0.9,1,0.1] regions.infanta [0.37,0.625,0.23,0.3] regions.infanta_face [0.44,0.645,0.06,0.065] regions.maid_left [0.18,0.625,0.2,0.3] regions.maid_right [0.55,0.55,0.23,0.34] regions.maid_right_face [0.61,0.555,0.06,0.065] regions.mari [0.755,0.615,0.165,0.31] regions.mari_face [0.8,0.63,0.06,0.07] regions.mirror [0.362,0.5,0.115,0.13] regions.mirror_glass [0.387,0.524,0.065,0.08] regions.nicolas [0.905,0.665,0.09,0.2] regions.nieto [0.535,0.52,0.07,0.13] regions.painter [0.15,0.495,0.16,0.33] regions.painter_face [0.2,0.505,0.06,0.06] regions.royals [0.388,0.548,0.062,0.056] regions.santiago [0.215,0.565,0.045,0.05]

And our question is: who is the painter painting?
    point at.ref art:painter_face gesture glow

    camera on ["art@[0.05,0.47,0.9,0.5]"] zoom 1.7

The answer starts with the (@highlight art:painter strength strong lift true@) painter himself: that's Velázquez on the left, brush raised,

stepping ba (@highlight art:canvas strength strong lift true@)ck from a huge canvas we see only from behind.

In the middle, in full light, stands the king's five-year-old daughter, the Infanta Margarita.
    highlight art:infanta lift true

Two young ladies-in-waiting, known at cour (@highlight art:maid_left art:maid_right art:cup@)t by the Portuguese word meninas, fuss over her; one kneels to offer a drink in a little red cup.

To the right stand two co (@highlight art:mari strength strong lift true@)urt dwarfs, Mari Bárbola

and Nicolás Pertusat (@highlight art:nicolas strength strong lift true@)o, who nudges a sleepy mastiff with his foot.

    camera reset true

Behind them, her chaperone talks (@highlight art:chaperone_guard art:nieto effect detail@) to a guard, and in the bright doorway the queen's chamberlain, José Nieto, pauses on the stairs.

Now follow their eyes: the painter, the princess, a menina, the dwarf. They all look out of the picture, at one spot.
    highlight art:painter_face art:infanta_face art:maid_right_face art:mari_face strength strong

    camera reset true

That spot is where we are standing.
    focus art:eyes_band strength strong rest grey lift true

    ask "Find the clue. Tap it." spot mirror on art
        intro: So who stands there? Velázquez left one clue in the room.
        right: Yes: the mirror on the back wall.
        wrong: Look on the back wall for something that could show the front of the room.

In the mirror, faint and blurred, are King Philip the Fourth and Queen Mariana.
    highlight art:royals effect detail

    pause 0.6

So the king and queen stand just  (@highlight art:mirror strength strong lift true@)in front of the scene, in our place, and the room has turned to look at them.

Perhaps they are  (@highlight art:canvas strength strong lift true@)posing for their portrait, and their daughter has come in to watch.

Some historians read the mirror differently: it may  (@highlight art:mirror art:canvas lift true@)show the front of that big canvas, with the royal couple already painted on it.

Either way, the true subjects of this picture are outside it, and we see the room through their eyes.
    highlight art:mirror_glass lift true

It's the trick of van Eyck's little round mirror, which showed two visitors on our side of the room; this one puts a king there.
    highlight art:mirror_glass effect detail

The red cross on the painter's chest is the badge of the Order of Santiago, a knighthood he received only in 1659, three years after the painting.
    highlight art:santiago effect detail

His early biographer Palomino says the king had it added after the painter's death: it makes him a nobleman, and painting, long seen as a mere craft, a noble art.
    highlight art:painter lift true

The picture hung in the king's private rooms, so the viewer it was made for was Philip himself, standing just where the mirror shows him.
    highlight art:royals effect detail

Today, whoever stands there takes the king's plac (@highlight art:painter_face strength strong lift true@)e: the painter looks up from his canvas,

and the whole room looks at you.
    focus art:eyes_band strength strong rest grey lift true

    quiz "A photographer wants a wedding photo that puts the viewer in the couple's place. Which follows Velázquez?"
        right: The guests look out at you, and the reflection shows who stands where you stand.
        wrong: Ask where the couple must stand for the guests to look at you, and how you could still see them.
        * The couple in the centre, smiling at the lens
        * The couple from behind, facing the guests
        + Guests look into the lens; the couple only as a reflection
        * Guests look into the lens; the couple among them, back to us

So who is the painter painting? Whoever stands where you are standing now.
    highlight art:infanta_face lift true

